  <h2>What a construction budget is</h2>
  <p>
    A construction budget — a job cost report, once the job is running — is the cost side of the
    project by cost code: what you planned to spend, what you have committed, what you have spent,
    and what you now expect to spend by the end. Set against the contract value, it answers the only
    question that matters mid-job: are we still going to make the margin we bid?
  </p>
  <p>
    The numbers come from documents you already have. The original budget is the cost side of the
    <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">estimate</a> — direct cost by section, plus contingency
    and sales tax — with overhead and profit left out, because they are not job costs. Approved changes
    come from signed
    <a href="/templates/change-order/">change orders</a>. Actual labor comes from
    <a href="/templates/timesheet/">timesheets</a> and payroll; actual material and subcontract cost from
    invoices; committed cost from the purchase orders and subcontracts you have issued. The template is
    where they meet.
  </p>

  <h2>Column by column</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Column</th><th>What goes in it</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td>Original budget</td><td
          >Cost from the estimate, by code. Never changes after the job starts</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Approved changes</td><td
          >The cost (not the price) of signed change orders, posted to the codes where the work
          happens</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr><td>Revised budget</td><td>Original + changes — the number you are managing to</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Committed</td><td>POs and subcontracts issued, invoiced or not</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Actual to date</td><td>Posted payroll, invoices and receipts</td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td>Est. to complete</td><td
          >Your forecast of the cost to finish this code, where you have one (Excel)</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Projected final</td><td
          >Actual + estimate to complete; otherwise the largest of revised budget, committed and
          actual</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Variance</td><td
          >Revised budget − projected final. Positive is favorable; red is over</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr><td>% spent</td><td>Actual ÷ revised budget — compare it with % complete</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The contract block at the top does the same arithmetic on the revenue side: original contract +
    approved change orders = revised contract, and revised contract − projected final cost =
    projected gross profit, shown as a margin next to the margin you bid.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example: the Harbor Point mechanical job at 74% spent</h2>
  <p>
    The completed example is the mechanical subcontract that runs through our template set,
    snapshotted on September 17, 2026 with costs posted through September 11. The original budget is
    $388,960.00 — the total estimated cost from the
    <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">estimate template</a>’s example, spread over
    nineteen cost codes, with contingency ($10,848.06) and sales tax on materials ($16,509.94) as
    their own lines. Three change orders have been approved for $11,154.50 of price; their cost
    side, $9,699.56, is posted where the work happened: a relocated curb to crane, sheet metal labor
    and RTU accessories; a deleted water heater as a credit to plumbing labor; a condensate drain to
    piping, pipefitter labor and the core-drill line. Revised budget: $398,659.56 against a revised
    contract of $497,354.50.
  </p>
  <p>
    Committed cost stands at $257,687 and actual at $295,280, 74% of the revised budget. Projected
    final cost is $397,145.06, a favorable variance of $1,514.50, for a projected gross profit of
    $100,209.44 — a 20.1% margin against the 20.0% bid. The detail is more useful than the total.
    The rooftop units came in $1,860 under budget, which is the buyout saving from the
    <a href="/templates/bid-tabulation/">bid tabulation template</a>’s example. Plumbing labor is
    $1,358 under. But sheet metal labor is projecting $853.70 over, pipefitters $736 over and
    supervision $420 over, and those three lines are where the project manager’s attention goes this
    week — not the healthy total.
  </p>

  <h2>Cost codes: use the same ones on every job</h2>
  <p>
    The workbook’s Cost Codes sheet is a CSI MasterFormat-style starter list for a trade contractor:
    division 01 for supervision, equipment, permits, contingency and tax; 22 for plumbing labor,
    piping, fixtures and equipment; 23 for HVAC labor, ductwork, piping, controls, insulation,
    balancing and rooftop units; and a handful of 02–33 codes for the demolition, concrete,
    electrical and site work a mechanical sub touches. Edit it to match how you actually buy and
    track work — but then leave it alone. A code list that changes every job cannot tell you that
    plumbing labor runs 8% over on medical work, and that is the number that fixes the next
    estimate.
  </p>

  <h2>Budget vs. schedule of values vs. estimate</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Estimate</strong> — before the job; cost by section and the price you sold it for.
      <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">Template</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Schedule of values</strong> — the contract sum broken into billable lines; the revenue side
      you bill against on each <a href="/templates/aia-g702-g703/">pay application</a>.
      <a href="/templates/schedule-of-values/">Template</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Budget</strong> — the cost side, by cost code, tracked weekly. This template.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The three should reconcile: the estimate’s total cost is the budget’s original total, the
    estimate’s price is the SOV’s contract sum, and every approved change order moves both the SOV
    and the budget. When the revised contract on this sheet does not match the contract sum to date
    on your latest pay application, one of them is missing a change order.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes this template is built to catch</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Budgeting from the sell price instead of the cost, and reporting a margin you never had.
    </li>
    <li>Ignoring committed cost until the invoice arrives, and finding out at month end.</li>
    <li>Posting change-order costs to one line, so no trade ever shows as over.</li>
    <li>Updating monthly from the P&amp;L instead of weekly from payroll and invoices.</li>
    <li>Reading % spent without % complete beside it.</li>
    <li>Changing cost codes every job, so nothing compares with anything.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For the discipline behind the sheet, read
    <a href="/blog/job-costing-for-subcontractors/">job costing for subcontractors</a>, and pair the
    budget with the <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order template</a> so every approved change
    lands on both sides of it.
  </p>
